from pathlib import Path
import json
root=Path('/mnt/data/v13work')
src=root/'src'
data=src/'data'; pages=src/'pages'

def q(question, answer): return {'question':question,'answer':answer}

labs=[
('lab-001','L1','React + Spring Boot','Build a User List from a REST API','Build a small feature that loads users from Spring Boot and renders loading, success and error states in React.', 'Create GET /api/users returning a list of DTOs. In React, keep loading/data/error state, call the API in an effect, and render each state explicitly.', '''// React
useEffect(() => {
  fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/users`)
    .then(r => { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`); return r.json(); })
    .then(setUsers)
    .catch(e => setError(e.message))
    .finally(() => setLoading(false));
}, []);''','Start with the backend contract, then wire the React request. I would explicitly model loading, success and failure rather than assuming the network always succeeds.',[q('Why should the UI check response.ok?','fetch only rejects on network failures; a 404 or 500 still resolves the promise. Checking response.ok lets the UI treat non-2xx responses as failures.'),q('Where should the API base URL live?','Keep it in environment-specific configuration such as Vite environment variables, not hard-coded in components. The deployed value can point to the appropriate backend host.')],['Do not assume fetch throws for HTTP 4xx/5xx.','Do not expose internal Spring exception messages directly to users.']),
('lab-002','L1','REST API','Add Create User with Validation','Implement a POST /api/users flow with DTO validation and useful frontend validation/error handling.', 'Define a request DTO with @NotBlank and @Email constraints, validate with @Valid, return 201 on success, and map validation failures to a stable error response. React submits JSON and displays field errors.', '''public record CreateUserRequest(
    @NotBlank String name,
    @Email @NotBlank String email
) {}

@PostMapping("/api/users")
ResponseEntity<UserResponse> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateUserRequest request) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(service.create(request));
}''','I would validate at the API boundary even if the frontend also validates. Client validation improves UX; server validation protects the actual contract.',[q('Why use a DTO instead of accepting the entity?','It prevents persistence details from becoming the API contract and lets validation, field exposure and versioning evolve independently.'),q('What status should successful creation return?','201 Created is the conventional response for creating a resource, optionally with a Location header and the created representation.')],['Frontend validation is not a security boundary.','Do not return a database entity blindly as the public API contract.']),
('lab-003','L1','State Management','Build Edit Form with Server State','Load one user, edit it, submit the change and keep the UI consistent after success.', 'Use GET /api/users/{id}, controlled inputs, PUT or PATCH for the update, disable duplicate submits, and either update local state from the response or refetch the resource.', '''const [form, setForm] = useState({ name: '', email: '' });
const [saving, setSaving] = useState(false);

async function save() {
  setSaving(true);
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/users/${id}`, {
      method: 'PATCH',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify(form)
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    setForm(await res.json());
  } finally { setSaving(false); }
}''','The important engineering point is that the form is temporary client state while the saved user is server state. After a successful mutation, the UI must reflect the authoritative server result.',[q('PUT vs PATCH here?','Use PUT when the request represents replacement of the resource; PATCH is appropriate when the API intentionally supports partial updates. The contract should define the semantics.'),q('How do you prevent double submission?','Disable the submit action while saving and, for important commands, make the backend operation idempotent or use an idempotency key.')],['Do not rely only on button disabling for duplicate prevention.','Do not assume the submitted object equals the persisted object.']),
('lab-004','L1','Pagination','Build Server-Side Pagination','Create a user table that requests only one page from Spring Boot and displays page navigation.', 'Expose GET /api/users?page=0&size=20&sort=name,asc. Validate page size server-side and return content plus total elements or a cursor depending on the API design.', '''GET /api/users?page=0&size=20&sort=name,asc

{
  "content": [...],
  "page": 0,
  "size": 20,
  "totalElements": 248,
  "totalPages": 13
}''','I would never load thousands of rows just to paginate them in React. Pagination belongs at the data source when the dataset can grow.',[q('When would you use cursor pagination?','For large or frequently changing feeds where stable traversal matters and arbitrary page numbers are less important. A cursor based on deterministic ordering avoids deep OFFSET costs.'),q('What should happen if page size is 10000?','The backend should enforce a maximum, such as 100 or 200, to prevent a client from creating an expensive query.')],['Never trust client-supplied page size.','Use deterministic ordering so pages do not shift unpredictably.']),
('lab-005','L2','Search','Build Debounced Search','Build a search box that avoids sending a request on every keystroke and ignores stale responses.', 'Debounce input by about 300–500ms, cancel previous requests with AbortController, and make the backend query indexed searchable fields.', '''useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(async () => {
    const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/users?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, {
      signal: controller.signal
    });
    if (res.ok) setResults(await res.json());
  }, 350);
  return () => { clearTimeout(timer); controller.abort(); };
}, [q]);''','Debouncing reduces request volume; cancellation prevents obsolete requests from winning a race and updating the UI after the user has typed something newer.',[q('Why is debounce not enough?','Debounce reduces calls but does not guarantee response ordering. A slower older request can still finish after a newer request, so cancellation or request identity checks are useful.'),q('Where should search performance be fixed if SQL is slow?','Inspect the query plan and indexes. If the query cannot use an appropriate index or the search semantics need specialized indexing, fix the data access design rather than only changing React.')],['Do not concatenate raw search text into SQL.','Do not assume requests finish in the order they were started.']),
('lab-006','L2','Security','Add JWT Authentication','Protect a React page and Spring Boot API using a bearer JWT.', 'Login returns an access token. React sends Authorization: Bearer <token>. Spring Security validates signature, expiry and required claims before allowing the controller to run.', '''fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/orders`, {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}` }
});

// Spring Security conceptually:
// request -> bearer token filter -> JWT validation
//         -> authorities -> controller/service''','The browser should not decide whether a user is authorized. It can hide UI, but Spring Security must validate the token and enforce authorization on the API.',[q('Authentication vs authorization?','Authentication establishes who the caller is. Authorization decides what that authenticated caller is allowed to do.'),q('What must be validated in a JWT?','At minimum verify the signature with the trusted key, expiry and relevant issuer/audience claims according to the security contract. Then map trusted claims to authorities.')],['Never trust a role sent only by the React client.','Do not log access tokens.']),
('lab-007','L2','Error Handling','Build a Consistent API Error Contract','Make React handle validation, authentication, not-found and unexpected errors without parsing random Spring messages.', 'Create a stable error shape such as {timestamp,status,code,message,fieldErrors}. Use @RestControllerAdvice to map known exceptions and keep internal stack traces out of responses.', '''{
  "status": 400,
  "code": "VALIDATION_FAILED",
  "message": "Request validation failed",
  "fieldErrors": {
    "email": "must be a valid email"
  }
}''','A predictable error contract is an integration feature. The frontend should branch on stable codes/statuses, not on fragile exception class names or free-form messages.',[q('Where should the mapping happen?','At the API boundary, typically through @RestControllerAdvice. Domain exceptions can remain meaningful internally while the API exposes a stable external contract.'),q('What should 500 responses contain?','A safe user-facing message and a correlation/request ID if useful. Detailed exception data belongs in server logs, not the client response.')],['Do not expose SQL, stack traces or internal class names.','Do not make every error a 200 response with an error field.']),
('lab-008','L2','File Upload','Build a File Upload Feature','Upload a profile document from React to Spring Boot with validation and safe storage.', 'Use multipart/form-data, validate size/content type, stream or otherwise handle the file safely, store it outside the database when appropriate, and return a resource identifier rather than trusting the filename.', '''const formData = new FormData();
formData.append('file', file);
await fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/documents`, {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
  body: formData
});''','The file itself is untrusted input. The backend owns validation, authorization and storage policy. For large files, object storage and pre-signed upload flows can keep application servers out of the data path.',[q('Why not trust the extension?','An extension is client-controlled. Validate size and content characteristics appropriate to the file type and never use the original filename directly as a storage path.'),q('When would you use pre-signed URLs?','When large files should upload directly to object storage, reducing application-server bandwidth and memory pressure while keeping authorization controlled by short-lived signed URLs.')],['Never build a filesystem path directly from the uploaded filename.','Do not load very large files fully into memory unnecessarily.']),
('lab-009','L2','Transactions','Build an Order Placement Flow','Create an order command that writes the order and inventory change atomically in Spring Boot.', 'Use a service transaction around the database changes. Lock or otherwise protect inventory correctly, validate stock, commit both changes together, and publish an event only after the database state is safely persisted.', '''@Transactional
public Order place(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
    Inventory item = inventoryRepo.findForUpdate(cmd.productId());
    if (item.available() < cmd.quantity()) throw new OutOfStockException();
    item.decrease(cmd.quantity());
    Order order = orderRepo.save(Order.create(cmd));
    return order;
}''','The transaction boundary should match the business invariant: either the order and inventory reservation both succeed or neither database change is committed.',[q('What about sending Kafka inside the transaction?','A database transaction does not automatically make a broker publish atomic. For reliable cross-system delivery, an outbox pattern is a common approach.'),q('What if two users buy the last item?','The database must serialize the conflicting inventory update, for example with row locking or an optimistic concurrency strategy with a version check.')],['Do not hold transactions across slow remote HTTP calls.','Do not assume @Transactional covers external systems.']),
('lab-010','L2','Idempotency','Build a Retry-Safe Payment Command','Design a POST-like payment command so a client retry after a timeout does not create two charges.', 'Require an idempotency key, persist the key with the resulting command outcome under a uniqueness constraint, and return the original result when the same key is retried.', '''POST /api/payments
Idempotency-Key: 8f7c...

// Database constraint:
UNIQUE(user_id, idempotency_key)''','A timeout tells the client that the response is unknown, not that the server definitely failed. The server therefore needs a way to recognize the same logical command when the client retries.',[q('Where should uniqueness be enforced?','At the database level with a unique constraint, because application-level check-then-insert logic can race under concurrency.'),q('What if the first request is still processing?','The implementation should coordinate concurrent requests for the same key, for example by a unique record plus state, and return the finalized result once available rather than executing the business effect twice.')],['Do not use only an in-memory map for idempotency.','Idempotency keys need a defined scope and retention policy.']),
('lab-011','L3','Observability','Build Request Correlation End-to-End','Trace one user action from React through Spring Boot and a database call using a correlation/request ID and structured logs.', 'Generate or propagate a request ID, include it in server logs and error responses, and add timing around important boundaries. In a mature stack, use distributed tracing with trace/span IDs rather than relying only on a custom header.', '''// Browser
fetch(url, { headers: { 'X-Request-ID': crypto.randomUUID() } });

// Server log concept
{"requestId":"8f7...","route":"/api/orders","durationMs":182,"status":500}''','The goal is not to add random logging. It is to make one failing request reconstructable across boundaries without leaking secrets or personal data.',[q('Request ID vs trace ID?','A request ID is a simple correlation mechanism. A trace ID belongs to distributed tracing and can connect multiple spans across services, queues and downstream calls.'),q('What should not be logged?','Passwords, access tokens, secrets and unnecessary sensitive personal data. Log identifiers and structured metadata that help diagnosis without creating a second data-leak surface.')],['Do not log entire request/response bodies by default.','Correlation IDs should not be treated as authentication credentials.']),
('lab-012','L3','Deployment','Deploy React and Spring Boot with Environment Configuration','Prepare a production deployment where the React build points to the correct backend and the Spring Boot service gets environment-specific configuration.', 'Build React with environment variables for the API origin, deploy the static assets, deploy Spring Boot with externalized configuration and secrets, configure CORS for the actual frontend origin, and verify health/readiness.', '''# React build-time configuration
VITE_API_BASE=https://api.example.com npm run build

# Spring Boot runtime configuration
SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL=...
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod''','Frontend environment variables are generally build-time inputs, while Spring Boot configuration is commonly supplied at runtime. Secrets should stay server-side and in a proper secret-management mechanism.',[q('Why can a frontend variable not be a secret?','Anything shipped to the browser can be inspected by the user. Frontend variables are configuration, not a safe place for credentials.'),q('What should be checked after deployment?','Browser network calls, CORS, API health/readiness, database connectivity, logs, static asset paths and the deployed API base URL.')],['Never put database passwords in React environment variables.','Do not use wildcard CORS with credentialed requests as a shortcut.'])
]

debugs=[
('debug-001','L1','Network','React shows “Failed to fetch”','The page works locally but fails in production. DevTools shows the request is not completing normally.', 'Check Network first: actual URL, DNS/TLS, request method, browser CORS messages and whether the request reached the server. Distinguish a browser-blocked cross-origin request from a backend 5xx.', 'The first failing boundary is the browser-to-API connection. I would inspect the exact request before changing React code.',[q('If the API works in Postman but not the browser?','That strongly suggests browser-specific policy such as CORS, mixed content or a frontend URL/configuration issue. Postman is not subject to browser CORS enforcement.'),q('What if there is no request in Network?','Check whether the code path executed, whether the URL is malformed, and whether the browser blocked or failed before a normal HTTP exchange.')],['Do not immediately rewrite fetch code.','“Works in Postman” does not prove the browser request is valid.']),
('debug-002','L1','HTTP','React receives 401 after login','Login succeeds, but the next protected API call returns 401.', 'Inspect the protected request headers. Confirm the access token exists, is sent as Authorization: Bearer, has not expired, and is accepted by the configured issuer/signing key. Then inspect Spring Security logs safely.', 'Start with the failing request rather than the login response. A successful login does not prove the protected request carries a valid token.',[q('401 vs 403?','401 means authentication is missing or invalid. 403 means the caller is authenticated but is not permitted to access the resource under the authorization rules.'),q('What if the token is present?','Check token expiry, issuer/audience, signature configuration, clock skew and whether the expected authorities are mapped.')],['Do not fix a 401 by making the endpoint public.','Never paste production tokens into tickets or logs.']),
('debug-003','L1','CORS','Browser says CORS blocked','The backend returns data when called from a server-side client, but the browser reports a CORS policy error.', 'Identify the exact frontend origin and API origin. Verify Spring Security/CORS configuration allows the required origin, methods and headers, and that preflight OPTIONS requests are handled.', 'CORS is a browser enforcement policy. I would configure the backend to explicitly allow the real frontend origin rather than trying to disable browser security.',[q('Why can OPTIONS fail before the controller?','The browser may send a preflight request and the CORS/security layer must handle it before the business controller is invoked.'),q('Can React fix CORS?','No. React can send the request, but the server must return the appropriate CORS headers. A development proxy can hide the issue locally but does not replace production configuration.')],['Do not use allow-all origins blindly.','Do not confuse CORS with authentication or authorization.']),
('debug-004','L2','Database','API became slow after data grew','A query that was fast with 5,000 rows is now slow with millions.', 'Measure first. Inspect query latency, execution plan, row estimates, scans, joins, sorts and index usage. Check whether pagination and predicates are index-friendly and whether application code introduced N+1 queries.', 'I would avoid adding an index blindly. I want the actual query plan and production-like data volume, then I would change the query or index based on evidence.',[q('What if the query already has an index?','Verify the optimizer can use it effectively. A low-selectivity predicate, function on the indexed column, stale statistics or an inefficient query shape can still cause a scan.'),q('Could React be the problem?','It could amplify the problem by making too many requests, but if individual API requests are slow, inspect backend/database latency separately.')],['Do not diagnose SQL performance from source code alone.','Do not increase database resources before understanding the bottleneck.']),
('debug-005','L2','Concurrency','Duplicate orders after a timeout','Users report duplicate orders after clicking once; logs show retries around a slow endpoint.', 'Correlate request IDs and inspect whether the client or gateway retried. Determine whether the command has an idempotency key and whether the database enforces uniqueness. Separate duplicate HTTP requests from duplicate business effects.', 'The key question is whether a retry can safely repeat the command. I would make the business operation idempotent instead of assuming network retries are rare.',[q('Why can a timeout create duplicates?','The server may have committed the first request while the response was lost. The client cannot know whether the operation succeeded and may retry.'),q('What is the strongest duplicate defense?','A durable idempotency record or business uniqueness constraint enforced transactionally at the database boundary.')],['Do not simply tell users not to retry.','Do not rely only on UI button disabling.']),
('debug-006','L2','Spring Boot','API returns 500 but frontend sees little information','A production request fails with 500 and the browser only sees a generic error.', 'Use the correlation ID to find server logs, identify the exception, determine whether it is expected or unexpected, and check whether the API error handler mapped it correctly. Preserve the internal stack trace in logs, not the response.', 'I want the client to receive a stable safe error contract while the server retains enough diagnostic detail to find the root cause.',[q('How do you prevent leaking stack traces?','Configure exception handling so unexpected exceptions map to a generic 500 response while the full exception is logged with correlation context and appropriate access controls.'),q('What if logs are missing?','Check log level, centralized logging pipeline, instance identity and whether the request actually reached the service.')],['Do not return exception.getMessage() blindly.','Do not debug production by adding arbitrary verbose logging containing secrets.']),
('debug-007','L2','React','Old search results overwrite new results','Typing quickly causes results for an older search term to appear after the latest term.', 'This is a race condition. Use AbortController to cancel obsolete requests or track a request sequence/token and only commit the latest response.', 'The bug is response ordering, not just request frequency. I would cancel stale work and make the state update conditional on the current request.',[q('Does debounce solve it completely?','No. Debounce reduces requests but two requests can still overlap and finish out of order.'),q('What if AbortController is not available?','Track a monotonically increasing request ID and ignore responses that are not the latest issued request.')],['Do not assume network responses arrive in order.','Do not mutate shared state from stale async callbacks.']),
('debug-008','L3','Deployment','Works locally, blank page in GitHub Pages','The React app loads in development but deployed routes/assets fail on GitHub Pages.', 'Inspect the generated asset URLs and Vite base path. For a project site, the build base must include the repository path, and deployment must publish the dist output.', 'I would inspect the browser console and generated HTML first. A wrong base path causes assets to request from the domain root instead of the project path.',[q('Why does it work with npm run dev?','The dev server serves from root and handles module requests differently. The production build needs the correct public base for the hosting path.'),q('What else can break on client-side routing?','Refreshing a deep route can produce a 404 on static hosting unless the hosting setup provides a fallback or the app uses a routing strategy compatible with the host.')],['Do not change React components to fix a build base problem.','Do not assume a successful local dev server proves production asset paths are correct.']),
('debug-009','L3','Security','User can see another user’s order','A user changes /orders/123 to /orders/124 and receives another customer’s order.', 'This is an authorization/ownership failure. Authentication succeeded, but the resource lookup did not constrain access by the authenticated principal. Fix service/repository authorization and test horizontal privilege escalation.', 'I would treat this as a security incident. The API must enforce ownership or permission server-side; hiding IDs in React is not a defense.',[q('Where should ownership be checked?','At a trusted server-side service/repository boundary, ideally close enough to the data access that every access path enforces the rule consistently.'),q('Is an unguessable UUID enough?','No. It reduces enumeration risk but does not replace authorization. A caller who learns a valid ID still needs permission.')],['Do not rely on frontend route guards.','Do not equate authentication with authorization.']),
('debug-010','L3','Observability','Latency spikes only under load','The service is fast with one user but p95 latency becomes high during peak traffic.', 'Compare p50/p95/p99, CPU, memory/GC, thread pools, DB connection pool utilization, downstream latency, request volume and error rates. Use traces to find which span expands under load.', 'I would correlate the latency spike with resource saturation and downstream timings instead of optimizing the fastest-looking code path.',[q('Why p95 instead of average?','Average can hide a smaller population of very slow requests. Percentiles show the experience of slower users and are useful for latency objectives.'),q('What if the DB pool is exhausted?','Find why connections are held too long or queries are slow, and size the pool based on workload and database capacity rather than blindly increasing it.')],['Do not optimize from CPU alone.','Do not increase every pool size at once; that can amplify downstream overload.'])
]

arch=[
('arch-001','L1','Evolution','Monolith → Layered Spring Boot','Start with a single Spring Boot service and separate controller, service and repository responsibilities.','A controller handles HTTP concerns, a service owns business rules and transaction boundaries, and a repository handles persistence. Keep DTOs at the API boundary.','The goal is separation of responsibilities before adding microservices. Splitting too early creates distributed complexity without solving a real boundary.',[q('Why not put business logic in controllers?','Controllers become hard to test and reuse, and HTTP concerns get mixed with business rules. Service-layer logic can be invoked by multiple adapters and tested independently.'),q('When should you create another service?','When there is a meaningful business or operational boundary, not simply because a class became large.')]),
('arch-002','L1','API','Layered app → REST contract','Define stable resource endpoints, DTOs, status codes and error contracts before connecting React.','Treat the HTTP API as a contract: resources, methods, validation, response shapes, errors and idempotency semantics should be explicit.','A frontend/backend integration becomes easier when both sides agree on a contract before implementation details leak across the boundary.',[q('Why version APIs?','When a breaking contract change cannot be made compatibly, versioning gives clients a migration path.'),q('Should every field be nullable?','No. Model required versus optional fields deliberately and validate them at the boundary.')]),
('arch-003','L2','Security','REST app → authenticated API','Add Spring Security and JWT without putting authorization logic into React.','Authentication is enforced by the backend security chain; controllers/services receive trusted authentication context and enforce authorities/ownership. React only adapts UX.','Security is a backend invariant. The UI can improve usability, but the API remains the source of truth.',[q('Where does JWT validation happen?','In the Spring Security request pipeline, before protected controller execution.'),q('Where should role checks live?','At authorization boundaries such as method security or service checks, with consistent policies for sensitive operations.')]),
('arch-004','L2','Data','REST app → production database','Introduce PostgreSQL with migrations, indexes and transaction boundaries.','Use schema migrations as versioned deployment artifacts, inspect query plans for important paths, and keep transactions around business invariants rather than HTTP calls.','A production database is part of the application architecture, not just a connection string.',[q('Why migrations?','They make schema changes repeatable, reviewable and deployable across environments.'),q('When should you add an index?','When workload evidence shows a query pattern benefits from it and the write/storage cost is acceptable.')]),
('arch-005','L2','Reliability','Synchronous API → resilient service','Add timeouts, bounded retries, circuit breaking and clear failure behavior around remote calls.','Every outbound dependency should have a timeout. Retry only transient failures with bounded attempts and backoff, and avoid retry storms. A circuit breaker can stop repeatedly calling an unhealthy dependency.','Reliability comes from controlling failure propagation, not from retrying everything.',[q('Why can retries make outages worse?','Many clients retry simultaneously and multiply load on an already unhealthy dependency.'),q('What should never be retried blindly?','Non-idempotent commands and permanent failures such as validation errors. Retry policy must match operation semantics.')]),
('arch-006','L2','Messaging','Synchronous workflow → event-driven side effect','Move email/notification processing out of the request path using an outbox and message consumer.','Commit the business transaction and an outbox record together, then publish asynchronously. Consumers must be idempotent because delivery can be repeated.','Use asynchronous messaging when decoupling, latency or independent scaling is valuable—not just because Kafka is available.',[q('Why outbox?','It closes the gap between committing database state and publishing a message by storing the event in the same database transaction.'),q('Exactly once?','End-to-end exactly-once business effects are difficult across systems. Design consumers and commands for idempotent effects instead.')]),
('arch-007','L3','Scale','Single instance → horizontally scaled service','Make the API stateless enough to run multiple instances behind a load balancer.','Externalize sessions/state when necessary, use shared database/cache/object storage, and make readiness/health meaningful. Verify concurrency and database capacity before adding instances.','Horizontal scaling is useful only when the bottleneck can actually scale horizontally.','If sessions are local, use a shared session store or token-based approach. Also ensure scheduled jobs and consumers do not accidentally run independently on every instance unless that is intended.'),
('arch-008','L3','Observability','Service → production platform','Add metrics, structured logs, distributed tracing and actionable health signals.','Measure request rate, errors, latency percentiles and saturation; correlate traces and logs; distinguish liveness from readiness; alert on user-impacting symptoms.','Observability should answer what is broken, who is affected, where the latency/error occurs and whether the system is recovering.',[q('Liveness vs readiness?','Liveness answers whether a process should be restarted; readiness answers whether it should receive traffic.'),q('What makes an alert useful?','A clear symptom, meaningful threshold, owner and runbook path. Avoid alerts on noisy internal metrics with no user impact.')])
]

# Fix arch-007 structure
arch[6] = ('arch-007','L3','Scale','Single instance → horizontally scaled service','Make the API stateless enough to run multiple instances behind a load balancer.','Externalize sessions/state when necessary, use shared database/cache/object storage, and make readiness/health meaningful. Verify concurrency and database capacity before adding instances.','Horizontal scaling is useful only when the bottleneck can actually scale horizontally.',[q('What if sessions are local?','Use a shared session store or token-based approach. Also ensure scheduled jobs and consumers do not accidentally run independently on every instance unless that is intended.'),q('Does adding instances always improve throughput?','No. A shared database, connection pool, lock contention or downstream dependency can become the bottleneck.')])


def js_array(obj):
    return json.dumps(obj, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)

# Write data modules using JSON-like JS exports
(data/'buildLabs.js').write_text('export const buildLabs = '+js_array([
    {'id':a,'level':b,'subtopic':c,'title':d,'objective':e,'approach':f,'code':g,'delivery':h,'followups':i,'traps':j} for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j in labs
])+';\n', encoding='utf-8')
(data/'debugLabs.js').write_text('export const debugLabs = '+js_array([
    {'id':a,'level':b,'subtopic':c,'title':d,'symptom':e,'diagnosis':f,'delivery':g,'followups':h,'traps':i} for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i in debugs
])+';\n', encoding='utf-8')
(data/'architectureLabs.js').write_text('export const architectureLabs = '+js_array([
    {'id':a,'level':b,'subtopic':c,'title':d,'goal':e,'design':f,'delivery':g,'followups':h} for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h in arch
])+';\n', encoding='utf-8')

page='''import React, { useMemo, useState } from "react";\nimport { buildLabs } from "../data/buildLabs";\nimport { debugLabs } from "../data/debugLabs";\nimport { architectureLabs } from "../data/architectureLabs";\n\nfunction LabCard({ item, type, open, onToggle }) {\n  const isBuild = type === "build";\n  const isDebug = type === "debug";\n  return <article className="lesson-card scenario-card">\n    <button className="lesson-head" onClick={onToggle}>\n      <span className="q-number">{item.id.split("-")[1].padStart(3,"0")}</span>\n      <span className="lesson-title"><strong>{item.title}</strong><span className="meta"><i className="level">{item.level}</i><i>{item.subtopic}</i></span></span>\n      <span className="chevron">{open ? "⌃" : "⌄"}</span>\n    </button>\n    {open && <div className="lesson-body scenario-body">\n      <div className="explain-section"><div className="section-kicker">{isBuild ? "OBJECTIVE" : isDebug ? "SYMPTOM" : "GOAL"}</div><p>{isBuild ? item.objective : isDebug ? item.symptom : item.goal}</p></div>\n      <div className="explain-section"><div className="section-kicker">{isDebug ? "DIAGNOSIS" : "IMPLEMENTATION"}</div><h4>{isDebug ? "Find the first failing boundary" : isBuild ? "How to build it" : "Architecture"}</h4><p>{isDebug ? item.diagnosis : isBuild ? item.approach : item.design}</p></div>\n      {isBuild && item.code && <div className="explain-section"><div className="section-kicker">STARTER EXAMPLE</div><pre>{item.code}</pre></div>}\n      <div className="explain-section interview-script"><span className="script-label">INTERVIEW DELIVERY</span><p>{isDebug || isBuild ? item.delivery : item.delivery}</p></div>\n      {item.followups?.length > 0 && <div className="explain-section"><div className="section-kicker">EXPECTED FOLLOW-UPS</div><div className="followup-list">{item.followups.map((x,i)=><details className="followup-item" key={item.id+"-followup-"+i}><summary>{x.question}</summary><div className="followup-answer"><strong>Answer</strong><p>{x.answer}</p></div></details>)}</div></div>}\n      {isBuild && <div className="explain-section mistake-section"><div className="section-kicker">INTERVIEW TRAPS</div><ul className="mistake-list">{item.traps.map(x=><li key={x}>{x}</li>)}</ul></div>}\n      {isDebug && <div className="explain-section mistake-section"><div className="section-kicker">INTERVIEW TRAPS</div><ul className="mistake-list">{item.traps.map(x=><li key={x}>{x}</li>)}</ul></div>}\n    </div>}\n  </article>;\n}\n\nexport default function PracticalLabs() {\n  const [tab,setTab]=useState("build"); const [level,setLevel]=useState("all"); const [query,setQuery]=useState(""); const [open,setOpen]=useState({});\n  const source=tab==="build"?buildLabs:tab==="debug"?debugLabs:architectureLabs;\n  const filtered=useMemo(()=>source.filter(x=>{const text=JSON.stringify(x).toLowerCase(); return (level==="all"||x.level===level)&&text.includes(query.toLowerCase());}),[source,level,query]);\n  const expandAll=()=>setOpen(Object.fromEntries(filtered.map(x=>[x.id,true])));\n  return <section className="learning-page">\n    <div className="learning-hero scenario-hero">\n      <span className="pill">V13 · PRACTICAL ENGINEERING LABS</span>\n      <h2>Stop only reading.<br/><em>Build, break, and reason.</em></h2>\n      <p>Turn React, JavaScript, Spring Boot and backend knowledge into practical engineering skill. Learn the flow, implement the feature, diagnose failures, and explain the trade-offs.</p>\n      <div className="learning-flow"><span>Learn</span><b>→</b><span>Build</span><b>→</b><span>Break</span><b>→</b><span>Debug</span><b>→</b><span>Explain</span></div>\n    </div>\n    <div className="learning-toolbar"><div className="search"><span>⌕</span><input value={query} onChange={e=>setQuery(e.target.value)} placeholder="Search labs, technologies, problems..." /></div><div className="filters">{["all","L1","L2","L3"].map(x=><button key={x} className={level===x?"filter active":"filter"} onClick={()=>setLevel(x)}>{x==="all"?"All levels":x}</button>)}</div></div>\n    <div className="subtopic-row"><button className={tab==="build"?"subtopic active":"subtopic"} onClick={()=>{setTab("build");setOpen({})}}>🛠 Build Labs <small>{buildLabs.length}</small></button><button className={tab==="debug"?"subtopic active":"subtopic"} onClick={()=>{setTab("debug");setOpen({})}}>🐛 Debug Labs <small>{debugLabs.length}</small></button><button className={tab==="architecture"?"subtopic active":"subtopic"} onClick={()=>{setTab("architecture");setOpen({})}}>🏗 Architecture Labs <small>{architectureLabs.length}</small></button></div>\n    <div className="result-row"><span><b>{tab==="build"?"Build Labs":tab==="debug"?"Debug Labs":"Architecture Labs"}</b> · <b>{filtered.length}</b> labs</span><button onClick={expandAll}>Expand all</button><button onClick={()=>setOpen({})}>Collapse all</button></div>\n    <section className="lesson-list">{filtered.map(x=><LabCard key={x.id} item={x} type={tab} open={!!open[x.id]} onToggle={()=>setOpen(p=>({...p,[x.id]:!p[x.id]}))}/>)}</section>\n  </section>;\n}\n'''
(pages/'PracticalLabs.jsx').write_text(page,encoding='utf-8')

